{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And sweet.</b> This verse is joined to the one after it. He says of the light that it is sweet, yet it is not a thing to be eaten. The essence of this is because of the joining of the sensations in the place on the forehead, and it is the place that people call by a term that is known for one sensation applied to another sensation. Likewise: \"See the smell of my son\" (Genesis 27:27), and \"All the people see the voices\" (Exodus 20:15). The meaning of this verse being joined to the first is: let him always engage in doing good, and not engage in other joy."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The phrase 'the place on the forehead' refers to the medieval theory of the common sense (sensus communis) located in the brain's frontal ventricle, where different sense perceptions converge; the Hebrew reads halit-clumsily but the argument is anatomical-physiological."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:29:11.772971964Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}